WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.525 --> 00:00:09.692
(audience applauding)

00:00:10.983 --> 00:00:13.890
- As a matter of fact, I was so nervous

00:00:13.890 --> 00:00:16.683
if there is no one in this room, (laughs)

00:00:16.683 --> 00:00:18.690
(audience laughing)

00:00:18.690 --> 00:00:19.740
but...

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:20.670
Oh, amazing.

00:00:20.670 --> 00:00:22.110
Thank you for your coming.

00:00:22.110 --> 00:00:25.230
I am Se-hoon Oh, the Mayor of Seoul.

00:00:25.230 --> 00:00:29.250
Before we begin, let's have
some time for ice breaking.

00:00:29.250 --> 00:00:32.100
I prepared pop quiz about Seoul.

00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:36.180
Of course, we prepared
some gift for the winner.

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:37.170
Let's start.

00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:41.730
Okay, the first quiz about Seoul, 629.

00:00:41.730 --> 00:00:43.350
It's a little difficult.

00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:48.350
For your information, it is
related to the history of Seoul.

00:00:48.390 --> 00:00:49.947
Okay.

00:00:49.947 --> 00:00:51.600
- [Audience Member] Historical landmarks?

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:53.280
- Historical landmark,

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:56.730
oh, I'm sorry. (laughs)
(audience laughing)

00:00:56.730 --> 00:00:57.563
Okay.

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:00.072
- [Audience Member] Years that Seoul

00:01:00.072 --> 00:01:01.820
has been the capital city of South Korea.

00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:06.060
- Okay, she's very smart.
(audience applauding)

00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:08.070
And last, four.

00:01:08.070 --> 00:01:13.070
It's related to my
political life. (laughs)

00:01:14.830 --> 00:01:16.083
Okay. Okay.

00:01:17.240 --> 00:01:19.007
(audience member speaking faintly)

00:01:19.007 --> 00:01:21.120
Okay. Thank you. (laughs)

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:24.600
I served four terms for now.

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:25.950
Okay, thank you.

00:01:25.950 --> 00:01:28.440
Let's start the lecture.

00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:31.668
It's enough icebreaking, yeah?
(audience laughing)

00:01:31.668 --> 00:01:33.390
As you can see here,

00:01:33.390 --> 00:01:37.170
my political life has been
full of ups and downs.

00:01:37.170 --> 00:01:41.430
The green part, two
part, is my mayor's term,

00:01:41.430 --> 00:01:45.120
and the yellow part is
my political absence.

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:45.960
Interesting.

00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:47.700
To make a long story short,

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:49.680
there is 10 years of political absence

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:52.530
between the two times of mayor's term.

00:01:52.530 --> 00:01:57.530
When I began my mayor's term
for the first time in 2006,

00:01:57.870 --> 00:02:01.650
I was focusing more on
making Seoul more attractive.

00:02:01.650 --> 00:02:04.110
But after 10 years of absence, you know,

00:02:04.110 --> 00:02:07.800
I was doing my volunteer work

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:11.190
in Peru and Rwanda for one year

00:02:11.190 --> 00:02:15.960
and I realized I had to do
more for the needy people.

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:19.740
You are looking at the
current vision of Seoul,

00:02:19.740 --> 00:02:21.840
but today I'm here to tell you

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:26.070
how and what we do to
help the needy in Seoul.

00:02:26.070 --> 00:02:28.320
Do you know Korea is very famous

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:32.250
for the speedy industrialization
and democratization?

00:02:32.250 --> 00:02:35.910
This picture shows us some symbolic aspect

00:02:35.910 --> 00:02:39.270
of every advanced
country, including Korea.

00:02:39.270 --> 00:02:42.600
The serious gap between
the rich and the poor.

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:45.090
The problem is the rich get richer

00:02:45.090 --> 00:02:48.150
and the poor get poor even more.

00:02:48.150 --> 00:02:52.320
The discrepancy becomes
bigger and bigger day by day.

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:55.620
Frankly speaking, the
fact that I was selected

00:02:55.620 --> 00:03:00.620
as Champion Mayor for inclusive
growth by OECD recently

00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:03.510
gave me such a rewarding feeling.

00:03:03.510 --> 00:03:07.830
But at the same time, I was
also surprised to find out

00:03:07.830 --> 00:03:12.120
that OECD knew exactly what I was doing.

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:15.090
As a matter of fact,
there are so many policies

00:03:15.090 --> 00:03:16.290
for the needy people,

00:03:16.290 --> 00:03:21.150
but today I'd like to
explain a few major policies

00:03:21.150 --> 00:03:22.920
for the socially neglected.

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:27.030
This is the first solution
for the polarization issue.

00:03:27.030 --> 00:03:29.940
Don't you think the start
line should be fair?

00:03:29.940 --> 00:03:34.260
Everyone in this room knows
the importance of education.

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:38.160
In Korea, however, students
need to get extra help

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:40.770
from the private sector,
so-called crammers,

00:03:40.770 --> 00:03:42.570
in Korean, Hagwon,

00:03:42.570 --> 00:03:44.340
to go to better college.

00:03:44.340 --> 00:03:47.340
This is the main culprit
of the big discrepancy

00:03:47.340 --> 00:03:49.170
between the poor and rich.

00:03:49.170 --> 00:03:53.730
So it hit me, how can I
help those poor students?

00:03:53.730 --> 00:03:58.530
This brought me to the
academic assistance program

00:03:58.530 --> 00:04:00.510
called Seoul Learn.

00:04:00.510 --> 00:04:04.230
This assistance program
provides the needy students

00:04:04.230 --> 00:04:07.050
with famous instructors, online lectures,

00:04:07.050 --> 00:04:10.170
studying materials for free of charge,

00:04:10.170 --> 00:04:12.240
along with collegian mentors.

00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:16.380
So we are trying to increase
the number of participants

00:04:16.380 --> 00:04:18.300
for the program next year.

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:21.210
We've done it a little over a year now

00:04:21.210 --> 00:04:24.930
and it has produced fantastic results.

00:04:24.930 --> 00:04:29.550
A total of 461 participants
entered universities

00:04:29.550 --> 00:04:31.980
through this program this year.

00:04:31.980 --> 00:04:35.610
I strongly believe that if
I can change even one life

00:04:35.610 --> 00:04:36.840
through this program,

00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:39.540
that money is worth every penny

00:04:39.540 --> 00:04:43.410
and I will not spare
any money for doing it.

00:04:43.410 --> 00:04:46.544
This clip shows the effect of Seoul Learn.

00:04:46.544 --> 00:04:50.127
(Mayor Oh speaking Korean)

00:04:52.895 --> 00:04:55.761
(uplifting music)

00:04:55.761 --> 00:04:59.844
(pupil's parent speaking Korean)

00:05:12.029 --> 00:05:15.029
(everyone laughing)

00:05:32.114 --> 00:05:34.410
(everyone applauding)

00:05:34.410 --> 00:05:37.410
- How do you feel about the clips?

00:05:37.410 --> 00:05:39.783
It's such a rewarding experience to me,

00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:44.340
and I know there is someone
like this in this room.

00:05:44.340 --> 00:05:47.580
Here comes the real deal
for the socially neglected.

00:05:47.580 --> 00:05:51.390
It will change all the current
welfare system forever.

00:05:51.390 --> 00:05:55.500
If this safety income
system becomes successful,

00:05:55.500 --> 00:05:59.280
all the current welfare system
you know might be antiquated.

00:05:59.280 --> 00:06:01.980
In the current welfare
system for the poor,

00:06:01.980 --> 00:06:05.700
even if you have opportunities
to work more and earn more,

00:06:05.700 --> 00:06:08.310
you hesitate or you can't work

00:06:08.310 --> 00:06:11.010
because the minute you work more

00:06:11.010 --> 00:06:13.740
than the government's set limit,

00:06:13.740 --> 00:06:16.770
you will lose all the right to be aided.

00:06:16.770 --> 00:06:18.810
So in the current system,

00:06:18.810 --> 00:06:22.470
people will not want to work
more and make more money.

00:06:22.470 --> 00:06:25.710
They would rather stay in
the cycle of being poor

00:06:25.710 --> 00:06:27.750
and not work anymore.

00:06:27.750 --> 00:06:32.730
But in the new welfare system
that I am experimenting now,

00:06:32.730 --> 00:06:35.760
people in the poor boundary
will want to work more

00:06:35.760 --> 00:06:37.080
and earn more.

00:06:37.080 --> 00:06:39.090
This is the comparison picture

00:06:39.090 --> 00:06:42.390
between the current welfare
system and Safety Income system.

00:06:42.390 --> 00:06:45.810
Once they are selected
as help needing people,

00:06:45.810 --> 00:06:49.140
we will monitor how much
they make every month

00:06:49.140 --> 00:06:52.530
and supplement them
with the money they need

00:06:52.530 --> 00:06:54.600
if they don't make enough money.

00:06:54.600 --> 00:06:57.990
And we will stop giving them extra help

00:06:57.990 --> 00:07:00.990
if they make enough money to leave.

00:07:00.990 --> 00:07:04.410
But even though they are not
provided with extra money

00:07:04.410 --> 00:07:06.270
because they worked harder,

00:07:06.270 --> 00:07:09.060
their status of getting
help doesn't change

00:07:09.060 --> 00:07:12.450
until they actually graduate from it.

00:07:12.450 --> 00:07:16.350
This kind of experiment is
very common in many countries,

00:07:16.350 --> 00:07:18.660
many cities, including Seoul.

00:07:18.660 --> 00:07:23.490
This is the outcome of the past
one year of the new system.

00:07:23.490 --> 00:07:26.640
There is a picture of professionals

00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:29.280
commenting on our experiment.

00:07:29.280 --> 00:07:32.730
Now let's talk about
the medical care system.

00:07:32.730 --> 00:07:36.780
At this point in time, I
think I need to say something

00:07:36.780 --> 00:07:38.910
about Korea's healthcare system.

00:07:38.910 --> 00:07:43.739
I can dare to say that our
system is far better than yours.

00:07:43.739 --> 00:07:46.140
(audience laughing)

00:07:46.140 --> 00:07:49.637
Please don't kill me for this.
(everyone laughing)

00:07:49.637 --> 00:07:51.330
In our current healthcare system,

00:07:51.330 --> 00:07:54.960
the richer pay more and
the poorer pay less,

00:07:54.960 --> 00:07:57.720
but the poorer get far
more medical benefit

00:07:57.720 --> 00:08:00.810
from hospitals and doctors' offices.

00:08:00.810 --> 00:08:02.700
Even with all this benefit,

00:08:02.700 --> 00:08:06.570
as mayor of Seoul, I'm trying
to build more city hospitals

00:08:06.570 --> 00:08:09.510
for the people who are in need of help.

00:08:09.510 --> 00:08:12.570
This policy gives me another pride.

00:08:12.570 --> 00:08:15.930
Nowadays, people do not starve to death

00:08:15.930 --> 00:08:19.860
because our government is
taking such good care of them

00:08:19.860 --> 00:08:21.000
in terms of food.

00:08:21.000 --> 00:08:23.520
In my opinion, at this point in time,

00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:25.170
we need to do something

00:08:25.170 --> 00:08:28.380
about their will to survive on their own

00:08:28.380 --> 00:08:31.650
because it's far more
important than the food.

00:08:31.650 --> 00:08:34.500
I've tried to give those homeless people

00:08:34.500 --> 00:08:38.730
the opportunity to take some
liberal arts related courses

00:08:38.730 --> 00:08:39.810
at colleges.

00:08:39.810 --> 00:08:41.790
Upon finishing the program,

00:08:41.790 --> 00:08:43.740
they will be treated the same way

00:08:43.740 --> 00:08:46.170
as ordinary college graduate,

00:08:46.170 --> 00:08:50.490
the same graduation suit and
the cap and everything else.

00:08:50.490 --> 00:08:55.110
Believe it or not, about 70%
of participants in the program

00:08:55.110 --> 00:08:58.230
survived the courses and left the colleges

00:08:58.230 --> 00:09:01.590
with ever higher self-esteem.

00:09:01.590 --> 00:09:05.670
Yeah. Now let's take our
attention to the world.

00:09:05.670 --> 00:09:09.630
You know we had a horrible
conflict with North Korea

00:09:09.630 --> 00:09:11.340
about 70 years ago.

00:09:11.340 --> 00:09:14.940
If we hadn't received help from the UN,

00:09:14.940 --> 00:09:16.830
I wouldn't be standing here

00:09:16.830 --> 00:09:19.650
and talking to you like this now.

00:09:19.650 --> 00:09:23.880
We can't forget the very
much needed enormous help

00:09:23.880 --> 00:09:26.433
from other countries at the time.

00:09:27.510 --> 00:09:30.690
And we are the only country in the world

00:09:30.690 --> 00:09:33.270
that had help from the world before

00:09:33.270 --> 00:09:37.170
and is giving the help
back to the world now.

00:09:37.170 --> 00:09:41.910
This aid money we gave
wasn't much in the beginning,

00:09:41.910 --> 00:09:42.960
as you can see.

00:09:42.960 --> 00:09:44.430
But as of this year,

00:09:44.430 --> 00:09:48.210
we are increasing the amount
of aid money dramatically

00:09:48.210 --> 00:09:51.480
and also, at the same
time, we are sending out

00:09:51.480 --> 00:09:53.700
more volunteers to the world.

00:09:53.700 --> 00:09:56.700
You know, I'm one of those believers

00:09:56.700 --> 00:09:59.610
that it's far better to help the needy

00:09:59.610 --> 00:10:01.680
by teaching them how to fish

00:10:01.680 --> 00:10:04.470
and not just giving them a fish itself.

00:10:04.470 --> 00:10:06.240
What a coincidence,

00:10:06.240 --> 00:10:08.400
the initials for Knowledge Sharing Program

00:10:08.400 --> 00:10:11.190
are the same as Korea Sharing Program.

00:10:11.190 --> 00:10:14.670
I hope this transformation of Korea

00:10:14.670 --> 00:10:17.250
can give inspiration and courage

00:10:17.250 --> 00:10:19.620
to many less developed countries.

00:10:19.620 --> 00:10:24.360
This is what we are doing to
help underdeveloped countries

00:10:24.360 --> 00:10:26.670
become more self-sufficient

00:10:26.670 --> 00:10:29.400
by growing rice more productively.

00:10:29.400 --> 00:10:30.720
This is possible

00:10:30.720 --> 00:10:35.370
because Korea could develop
easier rice to breed

00:10:35.370 --> 00:10:39.270
to survive in destitute
African countries like Rwanda.

00:10:39.270 --> 00:10:40.800
We are making every effort

00:10:40.800 --> 00:10:44.220
to solve the starvation issues in Africa

00:10:44.220 --> 00:10:46.950
by developing new rice breed,

00:10:46.950 --> 00:10:50.070
and it has been very successful so far.

00:10:50.070 --> 00:10:54.600
I myself stayed in Rwanda
for six months in 2014.

00:10:55.890 --> 00:10:59.220
At the time, I helped the
city government of Kigali

00:10:59.220 --> 00:11:02.370
with the issues such as industrialization

00:11:02.370 --> 00:11:04.140
and welfare systems.

00:11:04.140 --> 00:11:05.610
While I was there,

00:11:05.610 --> 00:11:08.790
I was able to witness a
highly successful case

00:11:08.790 --> 00:11:11.340
of growing rice more effectively

00:11:11.340 --> 00:11:13.440
in the remote village of Musimba.

00:11:13.440 --> 00:11:15.090
In the new village movement,

00:11:15.090 --> 00:11:18.300
so-called Saemaeul Movement in Korea,

00:11:18.300 --> 00:11:21.630
about five volunteers worked together

00:11:21.630 --> 00:11:23.820
as a group for a few years.

00:11:23.820 --> 00:11:26.910
These volunteers led dramatic changes

00:11:26.910 --> 00:11:29.220
in the mindset of the villagers.

00:11:29.220 --> 00:11:33.630
They took the initiative and
set examples for the villagers.

00:11:33.630 --> 00:11:38.430
The locals slowly but surely
learned how to grow rice

00:11:38.430 --> 00:11:42.090
and, finally, they realized
the true meaning of diligence,

00:11:42.090 --> 00:11:44.340
self-aid and cooperation.

00:11:44.340 --> 00:11:45.810
These three principles,

00:11:45.810 --> 00:11:48.780
diligence, self-aid and cooperation,

00:11:48.780 --> 00:11:51.750
are the spirits of the Saemaeul Movement

00:11:51.750 --> 00:11:55.290
that were the fundamentals
of Korean development.

00:11:55.290 --> 00:11:58.170
On the other hand, this
is sharing knowledge

00:11:58.170 --> 00:11:59.760
and experience of Seoul.

00:11:59.760 --> 00:12:04.320
We are sharing our knowledge
and experience with 61 cities

00:12:04.320 --> 00:12:06.300
in 35 countries.

00:12:06.300 --> 00:12:09.210
These are various examples

00:12:09.210 --> 00:12:13.233
of sharing our best
practices around the world.

00:12:14.880 --> 00:12:17.610
According to the news, quite a few cities,

00:12:17.610 --> 00:12:19.410
including the city of New York,

00:12:19.410 --> 00:12:23.790
is benchmarking our way
of processing food waste,

00:12:23.790 --> 00:12:27.900
which is being turned into
fertilizers, animal feeds,

00:12:27.900 --> 00:12:29.730
and sources of energy.

00:12:29.730 --> 00:12:32.640
In Seoul, you can pay for one low price

00:12:32.640 --> 00:12:34.260
of public transportation

00:12:34.260 --> 00:12:37.320
and you can take the subway or buses

00:12:37.320 --> 00:12:39.300
as many times as you want.

00:12:39.300 --> 00:12:43.410
Actually, two weeks ago,
I announced a new policy

00:12:43.410 --> 00:12:46.890
of unlimited use of various
transportation systems,

00:12:46.890 --> 00:12:50.550
including buses, subways, and bicycles,

00:12:50.550 --> 00:12:52.890
and in one year, river bus,

00:12:52.890 --> 00:12:56.670
for only $50 a month by
benchmarking Germany.

00:12:56.670 --> 00:12:59.520
Its name is Climate Companion Card.

00:12:59.520 --> 00:13:03.390
As you all know, Seoul is
a world famous smart city

00:13:03.390 --> 00:13:06.300
with stronger infrastructure ever now.

00:13:06.300 --> 00:13:08.040
There's no question about that.

00:13:08.040 --> 00:13:12.510
And last year, we launched
the Seoul Smart City Prize.

00:13:12.510 --> 00:13:16.320
So far, 240 cities, corporations,

00:13:16.320 --> 00:13:18.540
and organizations around the world

00:13:18.540 --> 00:13:20.400
have applied for the prize.

00:13:20.400 --> 00:13:22.440
And shortly after I return to Seoul,

00:13:22.440 --> 00:13:25.740
the World Cities Summit
Mayors Forum will be held

00:13:25.740 --> 00:13:27.330
to award the prize.

00:13:27.330 --> 00:13:31.680
At last, this is the last
page of my presentation.

00:13:31.680 --> 00:13:34.203
Can any of you tell which one is me?

00:13:35.070 --> 00:13:36.093
Yeah, this is me.

00:13:37.110 --> 00:13:39.480
I was seven years old

00:13:39.480 --> 00:13:42.510
and I was with the hoodlums
in a poor neighborhood

00:13:42.510 --> 00:13:43.800
where I used to live in.

00:13:43.800 --> 00:13:48.030
Can you guess why I'm so
crazy about going together

00:13:48.030 --> 00:13:49.650
with the socially neglected?

00:13:49.650 --> 00:13:52.380
As a matter of fact, two days ago,

00:13:52.380 --> 00:13:56.400
I and the mayor of New York, Eric Adams,

00:13:56.400 --> 00:14:00.750
met and drank together.
(audience laughing)

00:14:00.750 --> 00:14:03.930
And at the time, we found
out many common things

00:14:03.930 --> 00:14:07.170
about our childhood background.

00:14:07.170 --> 00:14:10.627
At the time, he told me his mother said,

00:14:10.627 --> 00:14:13.290
"Hard time makes strong men."

00:14:13.290 --> 00:14:15.420
I totally agreed with him.

00:14:15.420 --> 00:14:19.820
It's one of main theme of
today's lecture, I think.

00:14:19.820 --> 00:14:22.987
(audience applauding)

00:14:26.850 --> 00:14:31.770
Actually, today's theme was
a little serious, isn't it?

00:14:31.770 --> 00:14:36.770
So I prepared a video clip,
one-minute shot about Seoul.

00:14:37.209 --> 00:14:40.292
(upbeat jazzy music)

00:14:45.801 --> 00:14:48.884
(upbeat jazzy music)

00:14:49.900 --> 00:14:52.650
(train chugging)

00:15:16.304 --> 00:15:19.054
(crowd cheering)

00:15:27.436 --> 00:15:28.269
Thank you.

00:15:28.269 --> 00:15:33.269
Thank you for your listening.
(audience applauding)

00:15:33.815 --> 00:15:36.930
- So thank you, Mayor Oh.

00:15:36.930 --> 00:15:39.000
Let me test the microphone first.

00:15:39.000 --> 00:15:40.272
Can you hear me okay?

00:15:40.272 --> 00:15:41.105
- Yeah.
- Yes.

00:15:41.105 --> 00:15:41.938
- Okay, good.

00:15:41.938 --> 00:15:43.320
It's a real honor to be with you.

00:15:43.320 --> 00:15:44.730
My name is Erik Harms,

00:15:44.730 --> 00:15:46.980
I'm a professor of anthropology here.

00:15:46.980 --> 00:15:50.310
I specialize in urbanization
and Southeast Asia

00:15:50.310 --> 00:15:52.950
and have spent a long time
working in Vietnam myself,

00:15:52.950 --> 00:15:56.040
so I was very pleased to see
the interest that you have

00:15:56.040 --> 00:16:00.480
in the developing world
and your contributions

00:16:00.480 --> 00:16:02.070
to other cities.

00:16:02.070 --> 00:16:04.380
But I wanted to ask you a question

00:16:04.380 --> 00:16:08.010
about your emphasis on going together

00:16:08.010 --> 00:16:09.960
with the socially neglected.

00:16:09.960 --> 00:16:13.380
One of the themes that
most people talk about

00:16:13.380 --> 00:16:17.403
when they talk about Seoul
is high property prices.

00:16:18.840 --> 00:16:22.210
And it's difficult to reconcile

00:16:23.130 --> 00:16:26.250
the question of the socially
neglected with property prices,

00:16:26.250 --> 00:16:28.320
and so one of the things
that many housing activists

00:16:28.320 --> 00:16:31.320
will talk about is the need
to manage property prices.

00:16:31.320 --> 00:16:32.153
- Yeah.

00:16:32.153 --> 00:16:33.240
- On the other hand,

00:16:33.240 --> 00:16:38.240
oftentimes, those who think
about the national economy

00:16:38.370 --> 00:16:39.660
and the development of the city,

00:16:39.660 --> 00:16:42.723
some of the kind of images
that we see in the video here,

00:16:43.620 --> 00:16:44.580
will also say that,

00:16:44.580 --> 00:16:47.670
well, actually managing property
prices is very important

00:16:47.670 --> 00:16:51.540
for the stability of the
economic development.

00:16:51.540 --> 00:16:52.980
So I'd just like to ask you,

00:16:52.980 --> 00:16:54.810
what do you think about how to manage

00:16:54.810 --> 00:16:56.670
the property price issue,

00:16:56.670 --> 00:16:57.960
especially in conjunction

00:16:57.960 --> 00:16:59.430
with what you were talking about today

00:16:59.430 --> 00:17:01.283
with the socially neglect?
- Okay. I understand.

00:17:01.283 --> 00:17:05.040
I think it's just supply and demand issue.

00:17:05.040 --> 00:17:07.530
Actually, for the last 10 years,

00:17:07.530 --> 00:17:10.321
of course, it's another mayor's term,

00:17:10.321 --> 00:17:11.663
(everyone laughing)
not my term.

00:17:14.151 --> 00:17:17.910
At the time, he has a
kind of political status

00:17:17.910 --> 00:17:22.380
about the real estate,
housing, anti-redevelopment,

00:17:22.380 --> 00:17:23.940
rebuilding the houses.

00:17:23.940 --> 00:17:27.390
So because of the policy at the time,

00:17:27.390 --> 00:17:30.090
the price of houses are increasing

00:17:30.090 --> 00:17:31.860
and increasing day by day.

00:17:31.860 --> 00:17:36.810
I think it is very important
for us to supply enough houses.

00:17:36.810 --> 00:17:39.480
So after I became the mayor again,

00:17:39.480 --> 00:17:42.810
I started to supply enough houses

00:17:42.810 --> 00:17:46.440
through rebuilding and redevelopment,

00:17:46.440 --> 00:17:50.250
because there is no vacant
spaces within the city proffer.

00:17:50.250 --> 00:17:54.870
So if we want more supply of new houses,

00:17:54.870 --> 00:17:57.840
we must demolish old houses.

00:17:57.840 --> 00:17:58.980
And instead of that,

00:17:58.980 --> 00:18:02.910
we must build some more houses speedily.

00:18:02.910 --> 00:18:05.940
It's the most important solution.

00:18:05.940 --> 00:18:08.010
Of course, there are so many reasons

00:18:08.010 --> 00:18:09.930
of high price of houses,

00:18:09.930 --> 00:18:11.730
but this, in this short time,

00:18:11.730 --> 00:18:15.060
I can't explain all the reasons.

00:18:15.060 --> 00:18:19.560
First of all, the most important
thing is supply of houses,

00:18:19.560 --> 00:18:23.613
and I'm making every effort
to supply the new houses.

00:18:25.140 --> 00:18:26.520
- Thank you.

00:18:26.520 --> 00:18:28.110
I'll shift to another question.

00:18:28.110 --> 00:18:30.210
And by the way, after
I ask a few questions,

00:18:30.210 --> 00:18:31.860
we'll be opening it up to the audience

00:18:31.860 --> 00:18:33.333
for other questions as well.

00:18:34.800 --> 00:18:37.590
Another topic that one
immediately encounters

00:18:37.590 --> 00:18:40.950
when one thinks about
modern day South Korea,

00:18:40.950 --> 00:18:44.190
Seoul and modern day
South Korea as a whole

00:18:44.190 --> 00:18:47.412
is the concern about
declining fertility rates.

00:18:47.412 --> 00:18:48.800
- (chuckles) Yeah.
(audience chuckling)

00:18:48.800 --> 00:18:49.704
It's very serious.

00:18:49.704 --> 00:18:50.730
- It's very serious, right?

00:18:50.730 --> 00:18:53.340
And some scholars have speculated

00:18:53.340 --> 00:18:56.580
that some of the meteoric rise of Seoul,

00:18:56.580 --> 00:18:59.520
like South Korea's
economy over the past year

00:18:59.520 --> 00:19:01.620
has been related to demographic change

00:19:01.620 --> 00:19:05.250
and, in fact, limiting population growth.

00:19:05.250 --> 00:19:06.083
- Yeah.
- But now,

00:19:06.083 --> 00:19:08.070
people are very concerned
about the future.

00:19:08.070 --> 00:19:11.130
And so how do you imagine balancing that

00:19:11.130 --> 00:19:13.803
in a thoughtful way?

00:19:15.570 --> 00:19:19.320
- It's too tough question
because, as you know,

00:19:19.320 --> 00:19:24.320
there is no right answer
about the phenomenon.

00:19:25.020 --> 00:19:27.815
Let's ask to young people. (laughs)

00:19:27.815 --> 00:19:29.880
(audience laughing)

00:19:29.880 --> 00:19:33.260
Why... (laughs)
(audience laughing)

00:19:34.590 --> 00:19:37.380
Actually, there are so many reasons,

00:19:37.380 --> 00:19:39.750
but I think education,

00:19:39.750 --> 00:19:43.320
the cost of education is
very high in my country.

00:19:43.320 --> 00:19:44.940
Actually, as I told you,

00:19:44.940 --> 00:19:48.690
Koreans are very interested in education.

00:19:48.690 --> 00:19:50.193
Education is all,

00:19:51.630 --> 00:19:56.630
so they don't spare their money
to educate their children.

00:19:57.030 --> 00:19:59.130
So, many Korean young people

00:19:59.130 --> 00:20:02.670
feel that the cost of
education is too high.

00:20:02.670 --> 00:20:05.790
So the first solution is government

00:20:05.790 --> 00:20:10.790
or Seoul Metropolitan
Government must help them

00:20:11.310 --> 00:20:14.703
to educate effectively, efficiently.

00:20:15.540 --> 00:20:18.393
But as you know, it is almost impossible,

00:20:19.230 --> 00:20:21.570
so we are making every effort

00:20:21.570 --> 00:20:25.053
to help to educate their children.

00:20:25.950 --> 00:20:28.027
Anyway, immigration.

00:20:29.283 --> 00:20:32.340
Immigration is a kind of solution.

00:20:32.340 --> 00:20:33.570
It's very sensitive issue.

00:20:33.570 --> 00:20:37.824
So in Korea, I do not mention
about immigration. (laughs)

00:20:37.824 --> 00:20:41.340
(audience laughing)

00:20:41.340 --> 00:20:42.690
It's very sensitive.

00:20:42.690 --> 00:20:46.410
But recently in our country,

00:20:46.410 --> 00:20:50.463
the issue of immigration
is emerging as a solution.

00:20:51.750 --> 00:20:56.220
It's very beginning stage of
talking about immigration,

00:20:56.220 --> 00:20:59.163
but it start, it start.

00:21:00.210 --> 00:21:02.970
Maybe in one year, two years,

00:21:02.970 --> 00:21:07.800
many Koreans will agree with
the solution of immigrant.

00:21:07.800 --> 00:21:11.880
And moreover, the foreign students,

00:21:11.880 --> 00:21:14.100
there are many foreign students,

00:21:14.100 --> 00:21:17.280
especially Southeastern Asian countries.

00:21:17.280 --> 00:21:20.100
There are so many foreign
students in Korea.

00:21:20.100 --> 00:21:23.670
As I told you, there are so
many universities and colleges,

00:21:23.670 --> 00:21:25.050
54 universities.

00:21:25.050 --> 00:21:29.790
So we are considering
about giving them better

00:21:29.790 --> 00:21:32.040
to settle down in Seoul.

00:21:32.040 --> 00:21:34.590
It's very beginning stage of talking,

00:21:34.590 --> 00:21:36.240
but it's start.

00:21:36.240 --> 00:21:38.940
I think it's second solution.

00:21:38.940 --> 00:21:40.320
- So I'll have one more question

00:21:40.320 --> 00:21:42.000
before I open it up to the audience.

00:21:42.000 --> 00:21:43.860
I have other questions if it gets quiet,

00:21:43.860 --> 00:21:46.413
but I doubt it'll be quiet
in this great audience.

00:21:48.000 --> 00:21:50.040
The last topic I'd like to raise for you,

00:21:50.040 --> 00:21:53.850
you mentioned some of the
innovations that you have

00:21:53.850 --> 00:21:57.480
for addressing the environmental crisis

00:21:57.480 --> 00:21:59.250
that the globe is facing.

00:21:59.250 --> 00:22:03.360
And I have a colleague
here at Yale, Karen Seto,

00:22:03.360 --> 00:22:05.280
who's in the School of Environment.

00:22:05.280 --> 00:22:08.760
And one of the big
discoveries that she's made

00:22:08.760 --> 00:22:12.000
and has been sharing with
the world is, you know,

00:22:12.000 --> 00:22:14.430
we tend to imagine that cities

00:22:14.430 --> 00:22:17.550
are the opposite of
the environment, right?

00:22:17.550 --> 00:22:21.150
But in fact, cities themselves
play a very strong role

00:22:21.150 --> 00:22:24.060
in some of the global climate
solutions that we can have

00:22:24.060 --> 00:22:26.910
because cities have the capacity

00:22:26.910 --> 00:22:29.310
to bring people together to...

00:22:29.310 --> 00:22:30.960
Although the city itself may have

00:22:30.960 --> 00:22:32.370
certain ecological challenges,

00:22:32.370 --> 00:22:37.370
it can reduce environmental
footprints on a larger scale.

00:22:37.680 --> 00:22:41.580
Cities can be the space where one can have

00:22:41.580 --> 00:22:44.400
very strategic design interventions

00:22:44.400 --> 00:22:46.860
to solve the climate issues,

00:22:46.860 --> 00:22:48.390
and so on and so forth.

00:22:48.390 --> 00:22:51.780
You mentioned in your
talk about the innovation

00:22:51.780 --> 00:22:52.800
with the metro card,

00:22:52.800 --> 00:22:54.030
but clearly there's other things.

00:22:54.030 --> 00:22:56.070
And one of the things that people notice

00:22:56.070 --> 00:22:59.370
when they visit Seoul is they
expect it to be only city

00:22:59.370 --> 00:23:02.880
and then they see this kind of
very interesting integration

00:23:02.880 --> 00:23:04.830
with green space and city space

00:23:04.830 --> 00:23:06.930
that is actually quite well done.

00:23:06.930 --> 00:23:08.670
So I wondered if you
could tell our audience

00:23:08.670 --> 00:23:11.710
about some of the successes
that you see in Seoul

00:23:12.577 --> 00:23:13.740
and also some-
- Oh, success.

00:23:13.740 --> 00:23:16.890
- Some of the challenges that
you think need to be addressed

00:23:16.890 --> 00:23:19.380
and what are some of your ideas?

00:23:19.380 --> 00:23:20.213
- Yes.

00:23:21.090 --> 00:23:24.480
Frankly speaking, it is very difficult job

00:23:24.480 --> 00:23:27.390
for every mayor in the world.

00:23:27.390 --> 00:23:32.390
Actually, this visit to New
York is for the meeting of C40.

00:23:33.062 --> 00:23:36.930
C40 is the mayors forum in the world

00:23:36.930 --> 00:23:39.930
to solve the climate crisis.

00:23:39.930 --> 00:23:42.150
At the steering committee,

00:23:42.150 --> 00:23:44.100
when I attend the steering committee,

00:23:44.100 --> 00:23:49.100
I reported three experiences,
three issues in Seoul.

00:23:49.380 --> 00:23:54.000
First, yes, I mentioned
today the waste separation

00:23:54.000 --> 00:23:56.700
and recycling,

00:23:56.700 --> 00:23:58.860
it very successful case.

00:23:58.860 --> 00:24:01.260
And second, as I told you,

00:24:01.260 --> 00:24:04.980
I introduced the Climate Companion Card.

00:24:04.980 --> 00:24:07.770
And for your information,
it's for the tourist

00:24:07.770 --> 00:24:11.040
and foreign people also.

00:24:11.040 --> 00:24:13.680
It'll be very useful, I think.

00:24:13.680 --> 00:24:17.160
And third was the eco-mileage system.

00:24:17.160 --> 00:24:22.020
Actually, 10 years ago, in my
first term of Mayors of Seoul,

00:24:22.020 --> 00:24:26.430
I started, initiated
the eco-mileage system.

00:24:26.430 --> 00:24:29.010
As you can guess, so large.

00:24:29.010 --> 00:24:34.010
Citizen of Seoul can reduce
the energy and tap water,

00:24:34.680 --> 00:24:37.950
it will be, accumulate as a point

00:24:37.950 --> 00:24:42.510
and they can use the
point as a kind of money.

00:24:42.510 --> 00:24:44.763
So it was very successful policy.

00:24:45.630 --> 00:24:50.130
As far as I remember, from 2005 to 2022,

00:24:51.330 --> 00:24:55.127
we can reduce the CO2, (laughs)

00:24:55.127 --> 00:24:59.993
CO2 about 9.2%.

00:25:01.080 --> 00:25:06.080
It was very successful trial
and very successful initiative,

00:25:06.930 --> 00:25:11.930
so I reported the successful
case to the mayors of C40.

00:25:12.750 --> 00:25:17.750
As you know, the CO2 is
produced in the buildings

00:25:18.210 --> 00:25:22.380
and transportation in
city, especially city.

00:25:22.380 --> 00:25:25.860
So general energy buildings
are very important

00:25:25.860 --> 00:25:30.270
and low-emission vehicles
are very important.

00:25:30.270 --> 00:25:34.410
The two solutions are
very important in the city

00:25:34.410 --> 00:25:38.910
because there is no factories
in the city like Seoul.

00:25:38.910 --> 00:25:43.910
So our ambitious goal is
reduce 30% of CO by 2030,

00:25:46.710 --> 00:25:49.530
70% by 2040,

00:25:50.609 --> 00:25:53.610
and 100% by 2050.

00:25:57.300 --> 00:25:58.949
- Do you plan to be mayor that long?

00:25:58.949 --> 00:26:00.330
(everyone laughing)

00:26:00.330 --> 00:26:01.700
To see it through?

00:26:03.238 --> 00:26:08.238
- Yes, of course. (laughs)
(audience laughs)

00:26:08.640 --> 00:26:09.480
Yes, it's...

00:26:09.480 --> 00:26:12.553
Anyway, it's very ambitious goal,

00:26:12.553 --> 00:26:16.680
aggressive goal of Seoul
Metropolitan Government.

00:26:16.680 --> 00:26:19.750
Frankly speaking, (laughs)
yes, I agree with you.

00:26:19.750 --> 00:26:22.650
- Well, thank you for
entertaining my tough questions.

00:26:22.650 --> 00:26:24.630
I wanna now open it to the audience.

00:26:24.630 --> 00:26:27.930
I believe we have
microphones on either side.

00:26:27.930 --> 00:26:29.790
And if it gets quiet, I have questions,

00:26:29.790 --> 00:26:32.520
but I hope I don't have to ask them.

00:26:32.520 --> 00:26:34.410
I think we can start on this side.

00:26:34.410 --> 00:26:35.860
There's a person right there.

00:26:38.850 --> 00:26:40.380
- [Audience Member] I just
wanna say thank you so much

00:26:40.380 --> 00:26:43.230
for this talk today, it's meant a lot.

00:26:43.230 --> 00:26:45.960
And personally, I thought your philosophy

00:26:45.960 --> 00:26:49.260
in terms of helping the
economically marginalized

00:26:49.260 --> 00:26:50.100
very interesting.

00:26:50.100 --> 00:26:52.140
And I just wanted to ask,

00:26:52.140 --> 00:26:54.210
how are you planning on
implementing that philosophy

00:26:54.210 --> 00:26:55.980
in terms of socially marginalized groups

00:26:55.980 --> 00:26:58.590
such as women and gender
minorities in Seoul?

00:26:58.590 --> 00:27:00.620
And what initiatives can
we look forward to in Seoul

00:27:00.620 --> 00:27:03.210
in the coming years for...

00:27:03.210 --> 00:27:05.493
Oh, sorry, for women
and gender minorities.

00:27:06.360 --> 00:27:08.468
- [Mayor Oh] I think
your English is too fast.

00:27:08.468 --> 00:27:10.620
(audience laughing)
- [Speaker] Oh, I'm sorry.

00:27:10.620 --> 00:27:13.407
- So I need some help from...

00:27:13.407 --> 00:27:16.301
Ah, it's very sensitive question.

00:27:16.301 --> 00:27:17.910
(audience laughing)

00:27:17.910 --> 00:27:21.273
As a matter of fact, my
party is conservative party.

00:27:22.265 --> 00:27:26.220
- [Audience Member] Yep.
(everyone laughing)

00:27:26.220 --> 00:27:29.430
- Of course, women, women's case, I...

00:27:29.430 --> 00:27:33.960
10 years ago, I started
the policy for the women.

00:27:33.960 --> 00:27:38.960
The initiative's name was For
Women's Happiness project.

00:27:40.020 --> 00:27:45.020
So, for instance, I made
the women only parking lot

00:27:47.490 --> 00:27:48.540
in the basement,

00:27:48.540 --> 00:27:52.383
and there are so many policies for women.

00:27:52.383 --> 00:27:55.966
(Mayor Oh speaking Korean)

00:27:57.145 --> 00:28:01.228
(Mayor Oh and audience laughing)

00:28:02.201 --> 00:28:05.784
(Mayor Oh speaking Korean)

00:28:35.530 --> 00:28:38.311
(Mayor Oh and audience laughing)

00:28:38.311 --> 00:28:41.280
It's my answer. (laughs)

00:28:41.280 --> 00:28:43.140
- [Erik] Would you like
to call on people yourself

00:28:43.140 --> 00:28:44.816
rather than-
- Okay. Yeah, okay.

00:28:44.816 --> 00:28:46.440
Yeah.

00:28:46.440 --> 00:28:47.904
Yes, you.

00:28:47.904 --> 00:28:49.842
(audience member speaking faintly)

00:28:49.842 --> 00:28:52.842
(everyone laughing)

00:28:54.483 --> 00:28:56.100
- [Audience Member] It's my great honor

00:28:56.100 --> 00:28:57.540
that I can ask you a question.

00:28:57.540 --> 00:29:00.098
I'm a postdoc in Yale University,

00:29:00.098 --> 00:29:02.910
and I'm also father of one daughter.

00:29:02.910 --> 00:29:06.780
So before I coming
here, I'm thinking like,

00:29:06.780 --> 00:29:09.960
after like five or six years,
I may go back to Korea.

00:29:09.960 --> 00:29:11.790
But nowadays, it's my problem

00:29:11.790 --> 00:29:14.883
because nowadays living in
Korea is too competitive.

00:29:16.110 --> 00:29:19.440
Like buying the apartment or teaching,

00:29:19.440 --> 00:29:21.753
education-
- I'm sorry about that.

00:29:21.753 --> 00:29:24.360
(everyone laughing)

00:29:24.360 --> 00:29:25.890
- [Audience Member] In addition to that,

00:29:25.890 --> 00:29:29.190
living cost of Korea is too high, like...

00:29:29.190 --> 00:29:31.080
Well, most of the Korean postdoc

00:29:31.080 --> 00:29:33.750
want to be a faculty in Korea, I think,

00:29:33.750 --> 00:29:37.810
but the thing is that the
salary of the faculty is too low

00:29:38.765 --> 00:29:40.920
to live in Korea. (laughs)

00:29:40.920 --> 00:29:45.920
So I think many doctor or
postdoc would agree with me.

00:29:46.410 --> 00:29:49.890
So this would be the
reason why many Korean

00:29:49.890 --> 00:29:51.600
want to stay here.

00:29:51.600 --> 00:29:52.830
I think you are,

00:29:52.830 --> 00:29:55.930
you would be like next president candidate

00:29:56.820 --> 00:30:01.200
on election next year, right?
(audience applauding)

00:30:01.200 --> 00:30:02.433
So I really want to hear-

00:30:02.433 --> 00:30:04.410
- Thank you.
- Know about your opinion.

00:30:04.410 --> 00:30:07.110
And second is that, is similar thing.

00:30:07.110 --> 00:30:11.070
So in these days, many
brilliant Korean students

00:30:11.070 --> 00:30:12.870
just want to be a doctor,

00:30:12.870 --> 00:30:17.760
so another major is kind of neglected.

00:30:17.760 --> 00:30:19.950
So do you have solution about that?

00:30:19.950 --> 00:30:21.510
Because it would be associated

00:30:21.510 --> 00:30:24.420
with our country's development, right?

00:30:24.420 --> 00:30:26.193
If everyone want to be a doctor,

00:30:27.600 --> 00:30:30.990
who would study about the
engineering or other stuff, right?

00:30:30.990 --> 00:30:34.293
So my question is simply two things:

00:30:35.340 --> 00:30:39.030
how can you handle about
our competitive environment

00:30:39.030 --> 00:30:39.990
or culture?

00:30:39.990 --> 00:30:41.520
Second thing is that,

00:30:41.520 --> 00:30:45.120
how can you serve those tactile origin?

00:30:45.120 --> 00:30:46.920
I mean, everyone wants to be a doctor,

00:30:46.920 --> 00:30:49.410
so how can you handle that question?

00:30:49.410 --> 00:30:52.200
Can I deliver clearly?
